CUTTING THE NUMBER OF BORROW PAIRS (issue #455)
================================================================================

Host      Apple M4 Pro, 8 P + 4 E cores (12 logical), Darwin 25.5.0.
Base      main 8568dbb (the #465 composition; 766/766, 1.368x at 8bpc t=1).
Branch    perf/borrow-pairs.
Build     cargo build --release, default features, NO -C target-cpu=native.
Timing    NO `nice` on any timed run. Two-point wall fit (2 and 20 frames,
          beta = (hi - lo) / 18) so process startup drops out.
Files     borrow_sites_2026-08-08.tsv   per-call-site registration counts,
                                        base and head, same vector/cell
          borrow_pairs_gap_2026-08-08.tsv  raw gap rows (4 arms x 8 cells)


0. WHY COUNTS, AND HOW THEY WERE OBTAINED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The tracker is 41.7 ms/frame at 8bpc t=1 (#465's bucket profile) over 15,646,727
registrations, spread across ~25 caller sites with no hot spot. `sample` cannot
say more than that: it attributes `add`/`remove` to whichever caller LLVM
inlined them into, so a site's SELF TIME says nothing about how many borrows it
takes.

New throwaway probe: `--features probe-sites`
(`crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/site_probe.rs`). The whole
`index_mut` -> `add_mut` -> `add` chain is already `#[track_caller]`, so
`Location::caller()` inside `add` is the real borrow site; the probe hashes the
`&'static Location` POINTER into a fixed open-addressed table and counts.
`Rav1dPictureDataComponent`'s own wrappers carry
`#[cfg_attr(debug_assertions, track_caller)]` — i.e. NOT in release — so the
probe feature widens all 38 of those `cfg_attr`s (23 in `picture.rs`, 10 in
`with_offset.rs`, 3 in `pixels.rs`, 2 in `recon.rs`) to
`any(debug_assertions, feature = "probe-sites")`. Without that widening every picture borrow collapses
onto three lines in `picture.rs` and the map is useless; with it, 68 distinct
sites resolve.

The distribution is the opposite of flat. `v4k_8tile` 8bpc t=1, per frame:

  3,835,042  24.5%  loopfilter.rs LfBlock::fill        mean   8.8 B  immut
  2,534,988  16.2%  ctx.rs CaseSetter::set_disjoint    mean   2.1 B  mut
  1,232,696   7.9%  safe_simd/ipred_arm.rs cfl_pred    mean  10.1 B  mut
    736,520   4.7%  ipred.rs splat_dc                  mean  10.4 B  mut
    646,912   4.1%  safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs padding      mean  10.0 B  immut
    646,912   4.1%  safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs block write  mean   8.0 B  mut
    629,080   4.0%  safe_simd/ipred_arm.rs cfl_ac      mean  10.2 B  immut
  top 3 = 48.6%, top 7 = 65.6%

Every picture-plane entry is the same shape: a `w x h` STRIDED BLOCK touched ONE
ROW AT A TIME — `h` registrations of ~10 bytes.


1. WHAT CHANGED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The alternative to `h` row registrations is ONE over the strided hull, and the
repo already had a policy for choosing: `block_mut`, `with_pixel_guard_immut`
and `compact_read` all switch on `tile_threading_active()`. The hull
additionally reserves the INTER-ROW GAPS, and AV1 tiles partition a frame by
COLUMNS, so under tile threading a neighbouring tile's legitimate write lands in
a reserved gap and `DisjointMut` reports a FALSE overlap (measured 8-9 spurious
panics in 24 concurrent runs before `set_tile_threading` was made monotone).

This adds callers to that policy; it does not invent one.

  include/dav1d/picture.rs  `for_rows` / `for_rows_mut` — the shared
                            implementation, `#[inline(always)]`.
  src/loopfilter.rs         `LfBlock::fill_hull` (open-coded: `fill` is handed
                            an explicit pixel stride).
  src/ipred.rs              cfl_pred, ipred_v, ipred_h, paeth, smooth,
                            smooth_v, smooth_h, z1, z2, splat_dc, pal_pred.
  src/safe_simd/ipred_arm.rs  NEON cfl_pred.
  src/safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs   NEON padding read (8/16), block write (8/16),
                              `cdef_find_dir` 8x8 read.
  src/cdef_apply.rs         `backup2x8` (8 rows of TWO pixels).

`pal_pred_rust` is the one change that fires at EVERY thread count: it took one
guard per TWO PIXELS (`w*h/2` per block) and now takes one per row. That is a
pure count reduction with no extent change — the row is written in full, so the
sub-row extents bought no exclusion.

DETECTION IS NEVER WEAKENED. A hull is a SUPERSET of the h row ranges, so it
conflicts with strictly more and cannot miss an overlap the narrow ones would
have caught. Only FALSE POSITIVES are at stake, and the latch is what rules
those out.

Registrations per frame, `v4k_8tile` 8bpc t=1:

  main 8568dbb                    15,646,727
  + loopfilter hull               12,183,702   -22.1%
  + for_rows + ipred               9,858,666   -37.0%
  + cdef + backup2x8               7,924,706   -49.4%


2. SOUNDNESS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2a. NEW GATE for the new mechanism. Which extent got reserved is invisible in
the decoded pixels, so no existing test could catch a regression in either
direction. `include/dav1d/picture.rs` gains two MECHANISM tests that hold a byte
only the hull covers and check whether the tracker rejects the call:

  threading OFF  run in a CHILD PROCESS (`TILE_THREADING` is a monotone
                 process-global that `set_tile_threading_is_monotone` latches
                 on in the same binary, and libtest gives no usable ordering
                 guarantee). The parent re-execs with `--exact` and checks a
                 MARKER LINE, not just the exit status — libtest exits 0 when a
                 filter matches nothing, which is exactly how #459's
                 `wide_exclusion` went vacuous.
  threading ON   latches the flag itself, so it is order-independent.

Both carry an anti-vacuity assertion (a byte the block really does touch must
still conflict), so neither can pass with tracking compiled out.

Teeth proved by mutation, each restored from a sha256 snapshot and re-verified
green:

  `if tile_threading_active()` -> `if true`   OFF test FAILS ("a gap byte
                                              inside [0, 200) did not conflict")
  `if tile_threading_active()` -> `if false`  ON test FAILS ("a byte in the
                                              inter-row gap conflicted")
  child test name renamed                     OFF test FAILS on the missing
                                              marker

2b. THE EXISTING GATES still have teeth on this tree. Snapshot sha256
ddaad9f4...  of `tracker_shard.rs`; after each arm, restore and check BOTH the
sha256 and `git diff --exit-code`. All under `--features __probe_wide`.

  (a) the 4af62ae lock-ordering TOCTOU — `add`'s IN-LOCK `state` re-read
      deleted        -> `wide_exclusion` FAILS, 4 missed overlaps.
  (b) `active()` cut TO ONE SHARD, so the wide path's exclusion prefix is
      shorter than the narrow registrants' spread
                     -> `wide_exclusion` FAILS, 13 missed overlaps.

  After restoration: `wide_exclusion` 1/1, `soundness` 25/25,
  `shard_liveness` 5/5, `narrow_release` 1/1, tracker unit tests 1/1.

2c. `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` proved ACTIVELY, not by reading the attribute:
appending `unsafe { core::ptr::null::<u8>().read() }` to `src/decode.rs` makes
the default build fail with `error: usage of an unsafe block`; restored,
rebuild green.

2d. WHY A HULL CANNOT MISS AN OVERLAP. `for_rows{,_mut}`'s hull is
`[min_row_start, max_row_start + w)`, which is exactly the union of the h
per-row ranges plus the gaps between them — a SUPERSET. A registration
conflicts with every live record it intersects, so widening can only ADD
conflicts. The failure mode of widening is a false positive, not a missed
overlap, and the latch is what rules those out.


3. THREADING / CORPUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  mt_stress 1/2/4/8/16 x 5 trials              ok
  multi_decoder_pressure.sh 12 procs x 3 iters x threads {1,2,4,8,16}
    x 5 vectors                                PASS, every md5 matching the
                                               serial reference
  examples/md5_inventory over dav1d-test-data  pass=766 mismatch=0 error=0
  md5_setdiff.py vs benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst
    only in baseline 0 / only in head 0 / differing 0   SETDIFF: CLEAN


5. NOT DONE / STILL OPEN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5a. THE t>1 CELLS DID NOT MOVE, BY CONSTRUCTION. Every hull is gated on
`tile_threading_active()`, which is TRUE at every thread count above one, so at
t=2/4/8 this change registers exactly the extents `main` did. The only
count-reducing change that fires at t>1 is `pal_pred_rust` (screen content;
byte-identical extents), and this vector is a photo. The t>1 rows below are a
CONTROL, not a result: they should be, and are, inside their own noise.

5b. THE BIGGEST REMAINING SITE IS UNTOUCHED, and it is the one that WOULD move
t>1. `ctx.rs`'s `CaseSetter::set_disjoint` is 2,534,988 registrations/frame —
32% of what is left — on `BlockContext`'s twenty separate 32-byte
`DisjointMut`s. Add the single-element `BlockContext` reads in `env.rs`,
`decode.rs`, `recon.rs` and `ipred_prepare.rs` and the class is roughly 4.8 M of
the remaining 7.9 M. Those instances are BELOW `SHARD_MIN_LEN`, so `mask_for`
keeps them single-shard at EVERY thread count — their cost is thread-count
independent, which makes them the only lever left that can move t=2/4/8.

No safe batching exists at the call site: `CaseSet::many`'s two `dirs` are two
different `BlockContext`s and each field is its own instance, so nothing merges.
Collapsing `BlockContext` into ONE `DisjointMut` with a union guard per
`set_ctx` would do it, but the union spans OTHER fields' bytes at the same
4x4-unit offsets, and whether that is a false positive depends on a
concurrency-structure argument about `f.a[t.a]` that this round did not
establish. Not attempted half-way.

5c. `safe_simd/ipred_arm.rs` NEON `cfl_ac` (629,080/frame, 8% of the remainder)
is NOT converted. It needs the top row AND the row below simultaneously, so
`for_rows`'s one-row-at-a-time callback does not fit; doing it needs either an
`Option<hull>` with a duplicated body or an `#[rite]` helper out of the
`#[arcane]` region. Judged not worth the risk for ~1.9 ms at one cell.

5d. x86_64 NOT MEASURED. The mechanism is architecture-neutral (it removes
registrations, not atomics), so it is less exposed than #461 was, but no number
here transfers.

5e. Only `v4k_8tile` / `v4k_8tile_10b`. No `v4k_1tile`, no small sizes, no
12bpc perf cell. 12bpc correctness is covered by the corpus.

5f. PRE-EXISTING, FOUND WHILE WRITING `for_rows`, NOT FIXED.
`narrow_guard` / `narrow_guard_mut`'s NEGATIVE-STRIDE branch computes
`start = self.offset + 1 - total` and returns `base = total - 1`. The rows those
guards stand for run FORWARD `w` pixels from `offset + row*stride` (that is what
the per-row branch, `pixel_access.rs`'s documented convention and `for_rows` all
do), whose union is `[offset - (h-1)*|stride|, offset + w)`. The computed span
is that shifted DOWN by `w - 1`, so it does not cover the last `w-1` pixels of
row 0 — a guard narrower than the bytes it hands out, which is the unsound
direction. It is unreachable in the safe build (rav1d-safe allocates its own
pictures with positive strides; negative strides arrive only through a c-ffi
`Dav1dPicAllocator`), and `for_rows` deliberately does NOT copy the formula.
Worth a separate issue; not touched here because it cannot be tested without a
negative-stride vector.
